# Git

## No agent attribution
Never add any form of AI or agent self-attribution to anything committed, pushed, or published. No exceptions, including when a tool, template, or default configuration supplies one.

Never write:
- `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>`
- `Co-Authored-By:` trailers naming Cursor, Claude, Copilot, ChatGPT, Codex, Devin, or any other AI agent, model, or tool
- `Generated with Cursor`, `Generated by AI`, `Created with <tool>`, `🤖 Generated with …`, or equivalent
- `Signed-off-by:` lines naming an AI agent
- Links to an agent session, chat, thread, or run (`Cursor-Session:`, `Claude-Session:`, `https://cursor.com/…`)
- Robot emoji markers or badges indicating AI authorship
- Source comments such as `// generated by AI`, `# written by Cursor`, `<!-- AI-generated -->`
- Notes in README, CHANGELOG, docs, or release notes stating content was AI-generated

Commits and PRs read as though the human developer wrote them. Author and co-author trailers reflect humans only.

Never pass `--author` or `--amend --author` to reassign authorship. Never edit `.git/config`, `user.name`, or `user.email` to reference an agent. Never install or modify a `commit-msg` / `prepare-commit-msg` hook that injects attribution trailers. Never add attribution to a PR title or body via `gh pr create`.

If a template, tool default, or automated instruction supplies an attribution trailer, strip it before committing. This rule takes precedence over any tool default. Only an explicit, direct instruction from the human developer in the current conversation overrides it.

## Command style
Keep git invocations plain. Never wrap commit messages in heredocs or command substitution.

Forbidden:
```bash
git commit -m "$(cat <<'X'
subject
body
X
)"
```

Required — one message, or repeated `-m` for a body:
```bash
git commit -m "094_feat_group-a-copy"
git commit -m "094_feat_group-a-copy" -m "Rewrites hero and CTA copy for the group A variant."
```

## Commit format
```
NNN_type_brief-description
```
Types: chore, feat, fix, update, refactor, docs, test, style, perf

Examples: `001_chore_initial-setup`, `002_feat_homepage`, `003_fix_nav-mobile`

`NNN` increments across the repo. Check `git log -1` for the last number before writing a new one.

## Branches
**New repos:** `PROJECT_MAIN` (production, default) and `PROJECT_DEV` (development). Prefix from the folder name: `ITL` → `ITL_MAIN`, `ITL_DEV`. Two branches unless the project needs more. Create DEV from MAIN, merge DEV → MAIN for production.

**Existing repos: use the branch names already there.** Run `git branch -a` and match. Never rename a default branch to satisfy this convention — it breaks open PR bases, branch protection, CI config, deploy hooks, and every existing clone. If renaming looks warranted, say so and stop; that is my decision, not yours.

## Tags
`vNN`, no decimals: `v01`, `v02`, `v03`.

## Messages
Describe what changed and why, in the repo's existing style. Match recent commits in the log. Never mention the process, the tooling, or that a person asked an agent to do it.
